{%- set glossary = lovelockGlossary -%}
{%- set terms = glossary.terms -%}

<div class="callout source-callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Where the paper is</div>
  <p class="reading-citation">Lovelock, J. E. (1965). A physical basis for life detection experiments. <em>Nature</em>, 207(4997), 568–570.</p>
  <p>Two things live in <strong>Google Classroom</strong> for this block: the original PDF, and the <strong>adaptive version</strong> with the reading supports built into the text. Open the adaptive version there and keep this page beside it. This site references the paper rather than hosting it, because the paper is still in copyright and this site is public. Everything below is reading support, not a copy of the paper.</p>
  <p class="companion-note">There is also a <strong>standalone reading companion</strong> linked in Classroom: the same ten paragraphs, glosses, and word list as this page, in one printable page you can keep open next to the adaptive version.</p>
</div>

<section class="verify-banner" aria-label="About the translations on this page">
  <p>The translations on this page were generated by an AI and have NOT been checked by a human. If you speak one of these languages, you know more than the machine does. Found one that is wrong, awkward, or means something different in science than in ordinary use? Tell your teacher: corrections get your name on them, and a wrong translation you catch counts as a documented AI error.</p>
</section>

<div class="reading-controls">
  <div class="lang-select">
    <label for="lang">Definitions also in</label>
    <select id="lang" name="lang">
      <option value="none">none (English only)</option>
      {%- for code, label in glossary.languages %}
      <option value="{{ code }}">{{ label }}</option>
      {%- endfor %}
    </select>
  </div>

  <p class="reading-lede">The marked paragraphs carry the argument. The unmarked ones are still the paper; skim them, and notice what kind of writing they are.</p>

  <div class="toggle-row">
    <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="toggle-path"> Close-reading path</label>
    <label class="toggle"><input type="checkbox" id="toggle-gloss" checked> Plain-English gloss</label>
  </div>
</div>

<section class="reading-section" aria-label="Read this for Origin and Purpose">
  <h2>Read this for Origin and Purpose</h2>
  <p class="reading-sub">Five parts of the paper that are not the argument, but tell you where it came from and who it was written for. Find each one in the PDF.</p>
  <ol class="origin-list">
    <li><span class="origin-what">The title and author block</span> <span class="origin-why">Who wrote it, and from what institution.</span></li>
    <li><span class="origin-what">The <em>Nature</em> citation line</span> <span class="origin-why">Which journal, which volume, which year. This is the whole Origin question in one line.</span></li>
    <li><span class="origin-what">The Acknowledgements paragraph</span> <span class="origin-why">Who helped, and who is being thanked.</span></li>
    <li><span class="origin-what">The NASA grant sentence</span> <span class="origin-why">Who paid for the work. Hold this next to Purpose: the paper argues for what a NASA mission should carry.</span></li>
    <li><span class="origin-what">The References list</span> <span class="origin-why">What he built on, and how much of it is his own earlier work.</span></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="sic-note">Two scanning oddities to expect in the PDF: a <strong>tho</strong> for <em>though</em>, and a <strong>whore</strong> for <em>where</em>. These are artefacts of the scan, not Lovelock's writing, and they are themselves an Origin clue: you are reading a machine's copy of a 1965 printed page, not the page.</p>
</section>

<section class="reading-section" aria-label="The close-reading path">
  <h2>The close-reading path</h2>
  <p class="reading-sub">Ten paragraphs, in the order they appear. Each one is identified by its opening words: find that paragraph in the PDF, read it, then open the gloss.</p>

  <ol class="path-list">
    {%- set glosses = [
      {locator: "The design of an efficient and unequivocal experiment", extra: "with its two numbered points", gloss: "Lovelock says a good life-detection test needs two things first: a definition of life you can actually test for, and knowledge of what the planet is like now and was like in the past."},
      {locator: "It is the object of this article to show", gloss: "His claim: you do not have to guess what alien life is made of. You can look for what life DOES instead of what life IS."},
      {locator: "It is a relatively simple matter to distinguish", gloss: "On Earth, chemistry tests tell living from non-living easily. The weakness: they assume all life everywhere shares our chemistry."},
      {locator: "It is also possible to distinguish living from inorganic matter", gloss: "Physics can tell the difference too. A salmon swimming upstream looks purposeful in a way a rock never does. This test is less exact, but it works anywhere."},
      {locator: "Life is one member of the class", extra: "the indented classification quotation", gloss: "Scientists agree life belongs to a group of things that take in energy, use it to become more ordered inside, and push the waste back out."},
      {locator: "This classification is broad and includes also phenomena such as flames", gloss: "Flames and whirlpools do this too. Life is different in three ways: it is rare, it lasts, and it changes its surroundings enormously."},
      {locator: "The omnipresence of intense orderliness", extra: "and Extreme departures: the two numbered distinguishing features", gloss: "So a living planet shows two signs: order everywhere that random chemistry could not produce, and chemicals sitting together that should have reacted long ago."},
      {locator: "Ordinarily one does not look for fish in a desert", gloss: "You would not look for fish in a desert. So should we look for Earth-type life on Mars, or ask the general question instead? It depends on Mars's history."},
      {locator: "The following is the only sure information so far available on Mars", gloss: "What we actually know about Mars: dry, thin air, almost no oxygen, harsh ultraviolet light, sometimes above freezing. Maybe nitrogen oxides."},
      {locator: "If these conditions are representative of Mars", gloss: "If Mars has always been like this, its life (if any) would not resemble ours. So the general physical tests are the safer bet for an early mission."}
    ] -%}
    {%- for item in glosses %}
    <li class="path-item">
      <p class="path-locator">Starts: <q>{{ item.locator }}</q>{% if item.extra %} <span class="path-extra">({{ item.extra }})</span>{% endif %}</p>
      <details class="gloss" open>
        <summary><span class="gloss-eyebrow">Gloss: not the paper</span></summary>
        <p>{{ item.gloss | markGlossaryTerms(terms) | safe }}</p>
      </details>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="reading-section" aria-label="Word list">
  <h2>Word list</h2>
  <p class="reading-sub">{{ terms | pathTerms | length }} of these {{ terms | length }} words are on the close-reading path. Tap any word for a definition. Turn on <strong>Close-reading path</strong> above to dim the rest.</p>
  <ul class="term-list">
    {%- for entry in terms %}
    <li class="term-row{% if not entry.path %} off-path{% endif %}">
      <button type="button" class="term" data-term="{{ entry.term | termSlug }}" aria-expanded="false">{{ entry.term }}</button>
      <span class="term-pos">{{ entry.pos }}</span>
    </li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>

{#- One card per term. Hidden until its button is tapped; the language layer is
    filled in by reading-page.js from the data attributes below. -#}
<div class="term-cards">
  {%- for entry in terms %}
  <div class="term-card" id="term-{{ entry.term | termSlug }}" hidden>
    <p class="tc-head"><strong>{{ entry.term }}</strong> <span class="tc-pos">{{ entry.pos }}</span></p>
    <p class="tc-plain">{{ entry.plain }}</p>
    {%- if entry.note %}<p class="tc-note">{{ entry.note }}</p>{% endif %}
    {%- for code, label in glossary.languages %}
    {%- set translation = entry.t[code] %}
    {%- if translation %}
    <p class="tc-translation{% if entry.verifiedBy and entry.verifiedBy[code] %} is-verified{% endif %}" data-lang="{{ code }}" hidden>
      <span class="tc-lang">{{ label }}</span>
      <span class="tc-term">{{ translation }}</span>
      {%- if entry.verifiedBy and entry.verifiedBy[code] %}
      <span class="tc-verified">verified by {{ entry.verifiedBy[code] }}{% if entry.verifiedDate and entry.verifiedDate[code] %}, {{ entry.verifiedDate[code] }}{% endif %}</span>
      {%- else %}
      <span class="tc-unverified">unverified</span>
      {%- endif %}
    </p>
    {%- endif %}
    {%- endfor %}
    {%- if entry.flag %}<p class="tc-flag">{{ entry.flag }}</p>{% endif %}
    <p class="tc-links"><a href="/reference/glossary/u0-lovelock-1965/#term-{{ entry.term | termSlug }}">Also in: course glossary</a></p>
  </div>
  {%- endfor %}
</div>

<section class="print-appendix" aria-label="Printed word list">
  <h2>Word list: close-reading path</h2>
  <ul>
    {%- for entry in terms | pathTerms %}
    <li><strong>{{ entry.term }}</strong> <em>({{ entry.pos }})</em> {{ entry.plain }}{% if entry.note %} {{ entry.note }}{% endif %}</li>
    {%- endfor %}
  </ul>
</section>

<script>
(function () {
  var LANG_KEY = "ees-reading-lang";
  var cards = document.querySelector(".term-cards");
  var langSelect = document.getElementById("lang");
  var pathToggle = document.getElementById("toggle-path");
  var glossToggle = document.getElementById("toggle-gloss");

  /* Show only the selected language's translation row, in every card. */
  function applyLanguage(code) {
    document.querySelectorAll(".tc-translation").forEach(function (row) {
      row.hidden = code === "none" || row.getAttribute("data-lang") !== code;
    });
  }

  var stored = null;
  try { stored = localStorage.getItem(LANG_KEY); } catch (e) { stored = null; }
  if (stored) { langSelect.value = stored; }
  applyLanguage(langSelect.value);

  langSelect.addEventListener("change", function () {
    applyLanguage(langSelect.value);
    try { localStorage.setItem(LANG_KEY, langSelect.value); } catch (e) {}
  });

  /* One open card at a time, moved to sit under the term that was tapped. */
  var openButton = null;
  function closeCard() {
    if (!openButton) return;
    var card = document.getElementById("term-" + openButton.getAttribute("data-term"));
    if (card) { card.hidden = true; cards.appendChild(card); }
    openButton.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "false");
    openButton = null;
  }

  document.addEventListener("click", function (event) {
    var button = event.target.closest("button.term");
    if (!button) {
      if (!event.target.closest(".term-card")) { closeCard(); }
      return;
    }
    var wasOpen = button === openButton;
    closeCard();
    if (wasOpen) return;
    var card = document.getElementById("term-" + button.getAttribute("data-term"));
    if (!card) return;
    var host = button.closest("li, p") || button.parentNode;
    host.parentNode.insertBefore(card, host.nextSibling);
    card.hidden = false;
    button.setAttribute("aria-expanded", "true");
    openButton = button;
  });

  document.addEventListener("keydown", function (event) {
    if (event.key === "Escape") { closeCard(); }
  });

  pathToggle.addEventListener("change", function () {
    document.body.classList.toggle("path-on", pathToggle.checked);
  });

  glossToggle.addEventListener("change", function () {
    document.querySelectorAll("details.gloss").forEach(function (gloss) {
      gloss.open = glossToggle.checked;
    });
  });
})();
</script>
